\f1\fs22 In chill, Sunday morning sunshine, the hover-copt ascended to the St Hedges platform, passing by three of its hover pads and attendant prophets of Latter-Day Capitalism, and settled on St Hedges\'92 extensive east field with a hydraulic sigh. Gerry was already waiting, tablet in hand, his expression suggesting he\'92d found nothing good to report.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton alighted and took in the scene. The cathedral\'92s gold-tiled roof caught the morning sun, still magnificent despite the cheap nylon parachutes draping across it like exploded party balloons. One chute tangled in the guttering; another hung from the bell tower\'92s cross.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Apparently, the Elvi know how to sky-dive,\'94 Dunhilton said wryly. \'93Where would they learn such an ancient skill?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry shrugged. \'93The original Grayling cloning technology could encode certain experiences and skills into their base DNA. For example, it\'92s long been thought the Elvi could be passable actors.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That\'92s preposterous.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton cast his eye about and saw more parachutes over trees, among bushes, and trespassing on garden beds. They walked around the field, its black buffalo grass torn up in sods where dozens of boots trampled tracks from the lawn to the cathedral doors.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They came in from under the Layer,\'94 Gerry said. \'93Fixed-wing aircraft.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Planes that shouldn\'92t exist.\'94 Dunhilton hated surprises.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We\'92re still trying to determine how many. Defence estimates twelve to fifteen. They scattered after the drop.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The Air Force lost garbage-tip aircraft flown by hospitality clones.\'94 Dunhilton scoffed, and kept walking, then: \'93Where did they get taxing planes?\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Probably a garbage tip somewhere. Or multiple tips. There\'92s all kinds of old tech underneath the banana skins, if you know where to look.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton scowled. \'93Clever.\'94 He was never one to underestimate adversary. \'93Planes are faster than hover-copts, presumably.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Himalayan tour buses are faster than hover-copts,\'94 Gerry cracked.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton walked. The cathedral doors\'97imported oak, brass fixtures\'97stood open. Inside, the nave showed signs of controlled chaos. Pews had been pushed aside to make room for the crowd. Hymnals lay scattered. A sequin\'97blue, catching the light from the stained glass\'97glittered on the marble floor.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The Very Arch Business Bishop\'92s office has filed seventeen separate complaints,\'94 Gerry said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I don\'92t care about d\'92Molesto\'92s feelings.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Quite right, your Serene Corporateness.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They walked up the nave toward the dais. The gilded staff\'97the one the High Elvis had grabbed in the video\'97rested in its ceremonial stand at a haphazard, disrespectful angle. Dunhilton stopped at the spot where the High Elvis had stood, where the light from the stained glass had refracted through his sunglasses and sequins. Beams of coloured light played across the stone floor in geometric patterns.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He knew exactly where to stand,\'94 Dunhilton murmured.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Planned,\'94 Gerry agreed. \'93The timing, the light, the spectacle. All calculated.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93This wasn\'92t just gatecrashing a wedding reception.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No, CEO. This was a demonstration.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton picked up the sequin between thumb and forefinger. He held it to the light. It threw a blue spark across his flat, faintly alien salamander face.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The inauguration at Clambake is the real problem,\'94 he said quietly. \'93Crowning a High Elvis, worshipping Sarah Jacobson as their Holy Momma\'85 all that bunny love and seditious minds.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sedition,\'94 Gerry finished.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton let the sequin fall. It ticked against the marble. \'93Do you know what this building is, Commissioner?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry waited.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s a balance sheet made of stone. Every tile, every pane of glass, every gilded stick in every ceremonial stand says the same thing: we own this. We own the light. We own the songs. We own the God who blesses the numbers.\'94 Dunhilton straightened the gilded staff in its stand. \'93And a hospitality clone walked in here and took the light for himself.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry made no expression at all.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Cabinet meets in forty-five. You\'92ll brief them on the parachute assault\'97how it happened, how we failed to stop it. Then we discuss the Clambake situation.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Understood.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And Defence?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tell him to bring his resignation letter. He can decide whether to submit it or let me decide for him.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry made a note on his tablet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They emerged into sunlight. The parachutes fluttered in the breeze. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton boarded his hover-copt. Through the viewport, he watched Gerry walk back toward the cathedral, tablet in his hands, already composing his briefing.
